{"availability":"available","full":"wendaferreira296-hue/Mortal-Shell-II-Ascension-Menu","name":"Mortal-Shell-II-Ascension-Menu","description":"Mortal Shell II Trainer Menu 2026: 40+ God Mode, Fly, Teleport and One-Hit Kill Features Explained","category":"Developer tools","language":"HTML","stars":56,"forks":0,"created":"2026-09-21T21:56:00Z","updated":"2026-10-04T04:33:58Z","branch":"main","homepage":null,"topics":["cpp","hack","helper","menu","mortal-shell","mortal-shell-ii","mortalshell","mortalshell2","opensource","scripts","tool","trainer","utility"],"demo":"https://opensource.org/licenses/MIT","color":"#302e55","ink":"#dbd2ff","lastCheckedAt":"2026-10-04T08:17:17.598Z","lastAttemptAt":"2026-10-04T08:17:17.598Z","lastAvailableAt":"2026-10-04T08:17:17.598Z","metrics":[{"at":"2026-10-04T08:17:17.598Z","stars":56,"forks":0}],"technologies":[],"overview":{"kind":"readme","version":1,"paragraphs":["AetherShell Companion is an open-source, educational toolkit that explores the fascinating intersection of game engine analysis and runtime memory orchestration. Where the original concept focused purely on a menu overlay, this project goes further — it is a full laboratory for students, hobbyist reverse engineers, and curious tinkerers who want to understand how modern action RPGs like Mortal Shell II structure their entities, physics, and save states.","Think of it as a microscope for game internals: instead of simply flipping switches, AetherShell Companion provides the instrumentation, the language bindings, and the documentation needed to observe and reason about the systems running beneath the surface. Every toggle is paired with a diagnostic readout, every transformation is logged, and every hook is documented for the learner.","The project is intentionally modular. You can use it as a single monolith, or you can lift individual modules — the memory scanner, the overlay renderer, the Lua bridge — into your own educational experiments."],"features":["Runtime Modific